(* ============================================================================
   PART 0: LAYOUT LAYER (L0) -- LINE AUTOMATON (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   The block productions (PARTS 1-2) are context-free only OVER THE OUTPUT OF
   THIS LAYER. Indentation, container prefixes, and lazy continuation are 2D
   properties no string grammar can express (they need column arithmetic and
   carried container state); L0 states them as a deterministic one-pass line
   automaton instead of prose. The three engine implementations realize
   this automaton literally.

   INPUT.  Lines, split on `newline` as that production defines it below.

           A LEADING BYTE ORDER MARK IS STRIPPED -- NORMATIVE. A single U+FEFF
           at the very start of the document is removed before the first line
           is read, so `<BOM># T` is a heading and not paragraph text. ONE, and
           only there: a U+FEFF anywhere else is an ordinary zero-width
           character, and zero-width characters (U+200B, U+FEFF) are NOT
           whitespace and ARE ordinary destination characters.

           A NULL IS REPLACED BEFORE THE FIRST LINE IS READ -- NORMATIVE.
           Every U+0000 in the source becomes U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER,
           wherever it stands, before the first line is read. It is therefore
           not content, and it is the ONE C0 control PART 9 §29 does not make
           content; §29 carries that carve-out. EVERY occurrence is replaced,
           one U+0000 to one U+FFFD, so the transform does not change source
           offsets (carve#1523).

           Column arithmetic is PART 9 §24 C1. A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
           LEADING RUN (PART 7).
   STATE.  open := stack of open containers, bottom-up (document ->
           innermost), plus the current leaf (none | paragraph | heading |
           ...). Container kinds and the per-line prefix each demands:
             block quote    marker `>` [end of line | one space]
             list item      indentation to content_column (PART 9 §24 C3)
             footnote def   indentation >= 2 spaces; the prefix CONSUMED is
                            exactly 2, content_column := 2 (PART 9 §16)
             fenced body    (code / raw / comment / admonition / div /
                            line block / hard-break block) -- no per-line
                            prefix; bounded by its where-guarded fence pair

           EVERY FRAME CARRIES OWNERSHIP AND PARAGRAPH STATE SEPARATELY --
           NORMATIVE (carve#1730). A frame is the record

             owner              document | quote | list_item |
                                definition_body | footnote_body | fenced_body
             base_column        authored column at which this frame opened
             content_column     minimum column of ordinary child blocks
             container_open     whether the frame may own another line
             paragraph_open     whether its current leaf accepts lazy text
             last_boundary_kind ordinary | blank | line_comment |
                                fenced_comment | definition | heading | quote |
                                code_fence | raw_fence | colon_fence | table |
                                nested_list | continuation | end

           `container_open` is NEVER inferred from `paragraph_open`.
           `paragraph_open` describes an operation available inside a frame
           that has already survived; it does not keep that frame alive. S1
           and the transition table below select the surviving frames and the
           owner first. Only then may S3/S4 continue a paragraph.

           OWNERSHIP TRANSITIONS. `C` means the current container frame stays
           open after the boundary; `P` independently says whether a paragraph
           is open. `deepest P` means the last child container is followed to
           its deepest leaf. These transitions apply to list items, quotes,
           definition bodies and footnote bodies alike; a fenced body adds the
           opacity rule in S2.

             preceding boundary       container_open   paragraph_open
             ordinary line             C                yes
             blank line                C                no
             line comment              C                no
             fenced comment            C                no
             invisible definition      C                no, for full extent
             heading                   C                no
             block quote               C                deepest P
             closed code/raw fence     C                no
             closed colon fence        C                no
             table                     C                no
             nested list               C                deepest P
             continuation marker       C                deepest attached P
             end of input              no               no

           A closed block therefore has ONE effect in every containing frame:
           it leaves the frame open and leaves no paragraph open. A following
           line may close the frame because its column is owned by an ancestor;
           the closed block itself never closes its owner.

           ONE AUTHORED BLOCK BASE -- NORMATIVE (carve#1781). Every open
           container which accepts child blocks has a minimum
           `content_column`. A recognized block opener AT that column opens
           there. A recognized opener PAST it establishes its authored visual
           column as the local `base_column` for that ONE complete block; the
           opener, payload, structural continuations, metadata and closer are
           measured from that base. Canonical output returns the block to the
           container's minimum column. Ordinary over-indented text establishes
           no base.

           OWNERSHIP PRECEDES REBASING. The base belongs to the INNERMOST open
           container whose content column the opener reaches. While that block
           is open, a nested body's opener at its own content column is owned
           by that nested body; it MUST NOT be reinterpreted as a fresh sibling
           base in an ancestor. A blank line ends a paragraph, not the authored
           block which contains it. List items, definition bodies, footnote
           bodies and blocks attached by a continuation marker apply this same
           rule; their construct clauses only define the minimum column and
           attachment boundary.

           OWNER SELECTION FOR THE NEXT VISIBLE LINE. Determine this before
           interruption or lazy folding:

             column band                         selected owner
             past base_column but below          this surviving frame; the
               content_column                    residue is classified at its
                                                 local column zero
             at or below base_column             nearest surviving ancestor
             at content_column                   this frame
             past content_column                 this frame, with the authored
                                                 block base of §24 C3 when the
                                                 residue is a recognized opener

           The one qualification is a continuation claim already established
           by the PRECEDING ordinary line: an unblank-separated, noninterrupting
           line that misses a container prefix is assigned to the owner of that
           ordinary line. This is stored ownership, not a search for any open
           paragraph. After the owner is selected, `paragraph_open` decides
           whether the operation is continuation or a new paragraph. A blank,
           heading, definition, comment, table or closed fence establishes no
           such claim.

           Definitions register only in the context selected by this table.
           In particular, a definition below one body's content column does
           not register in that body; column zero belongs to the document or
           another surviving ancestor and registers there.

           COMMENTS ARE CLASSIFIED BEFORE BLOCK OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE
           (carve#1731). For each line, first honor any active code, raw or
           other opaque payload. Decode the active container prefix and
           authored indentation once. Then recognize `%%` and a matched `%%%`
           span as layout-transparent comment tokens BEFORE selecting an owner
           for visible block classification. Record the comment node, its
           authored spelling and its complete source extent, but remove it from
           visible classification and rendering. This is lexical erasure from
           layout, NEVER erasure from the AST or source map.

           Both comment forms apply the `line_comment` / `fenced_comment` row
           above: every enclosing container frame remains open and the current
           paragraph closes. A following blank repeats the blank transition;
           it does not close the container merely because a comment preceded
           it. The next VISIBLE line is owned solely by its prefixes and column:
           past a body's `base_column` that surviving body owns it, at or below
           the base an ancestor owns it, and at or past `content_column` the
           body owns it normally. The comment creates no lazy-continuation
           claim and cannot keep or close a container by parser fallthrough.

           A matched `%%%` token carries its opener, body and exact-width
           closer as one source extent across authored indentation. Its body is
           opaque. An opener without an exact-width closer is one `%%` line
           comment; later lines are classified normally. Comment delimiters
           inside code, raw content or another opaque payload are literal
           bytes. Nested containers, quotes, definition bodies and footnote
           bodies use this same phase ordering and transition without a
           construct-specific ownership exception.
   STEP (per line L, executed exactly once -- no backtracking, Design
         Principle 1: the contribution a line makes to block structure never
         depends on a future line, the only lookahead being the DECLARED
         &(...) closer guards on fence / frontmatter openers):
     S1 MATCH PREFIXES. Walk `open` bottom-up, consuming each container's
        prefix from L (strip `>` or `> ` for quotes; DEDENT by columns for
        indentation containers, PART 9 §24 C5). Stop at the first container
        whose prefix L does not supply.
     S2 FENCED BODY. If the innermost matched container is a fenced body, L
        is verbatim content unless it matches that fence's where-guarded
        closer (then pop). No other rule applies to L.
     S3 FULL MATCH (every prefix consumed). The residue of L continues the
        innermost container: BLANK -> close the open paragraph (recorded for
        tight/loose, PART 9 §17 L1); a BLOCK OPENER -> the interruption
        relation decides (PART 9 §10); a LIST MARKER reaching
        content_column -> sublist (PART 9 §24 C3); otherwise paragraph /
        lazy item text.
     S4 PARTIAL MATCH. The unmatched containers are candidates to close.
        OWNERSHIP FIRST: apply the stored continuation claim and the owner
        table above. If the line is owned by a surviving unmatched frame,
        NOTHING closes. Otherwise close unmatched frames through the selected
        owner and re-classify the residue there (S3). This decision reads
        `container_open`, `last_boundary_kind`, and columns; it does not read
        `paragraph_open`.

        LAZY CONTINUATION SECOND: only after ownership is fixed, if the
        selected frame holds an OPEN PARAGRAPH and the residue is NOT an
        interrupting line (PART 9 §10 -- list markers and plain text fold;
        visible openers interrupt), L folds into that paragraph. With no open
        paragraph, the same already-owned line begins a new paragraph in that
        frame. Lazy continuation therefore changes the leaf operation, never
        which container survives.

        NO STORED CONTINUATION CLAIM, NO LAZY LINE -- NORMATIVE. The ownership
        decision is not a leftover case, and it binds even where the unmatched
        container is a LIST ITEM whose last block is a container. In

            . >
            X

        `. ` opens an item whose content is an EMPTY block quote. `X` sits at
        column 0, so it does not supply the item's indentation prefix, and the
        quote boundary established no continuation claim. The owner table
        therefore selects the document: the item closes
        and `X` is a new top-level block. The same disposes of

            - >
            lazy

        while `- > q` / `lazy` FOLDS, because the ordinary quoted line stored
        a continuation claim whose selected leaf is an open paragraph. The
        owner is selected before that leaf is continued.

        THE MARKER LINE'S CONTENT IS THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#1280). EMPTINESS is not the property doing the work. The
        parameter is the one S4 names: does any container in the open stack
        hold an OPEN
        PARAGRAPH. A block that leaves none leaves none wherever it was
        written, and `- # H` writes a HEADING as the item's first block exactly
        as `- ` plus an indented `# H` would. So each of

            - # H          - | a | b |          - ---
            tail           tail                 tail

            - %% c         - [r]: /u            - [^f]: t
            tail           tail                 tail

            - {.k}
            tail

        ends its item and leaves `tail` a top-level paragraph, and each is the
        SAME derivation the block-quote spelling already gets: `> # H` / `tail`
        ends the quote in every engine today. Only `- > q` / `tail` and `- a` /
        `tail` fold, because those two are the ones with a paragraph open.

        A definition still gets collected from where it was written (SS17 L6);
        ending the item disposes of the FOLLOWING line, not of the definition.

        A FLOATING ATTRIBUTE BLOCK DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONTENT COLUMN --
        NORMATIVE (carve#1705, carve-rs#1373). In

            -{#item} {#next}
              # H

        marker-attached `{#item}` is item metadata and contributes zero to the
        content column (§24 C3), so `# H` at source column 2 is AT the bullet
        item's content column, not below it. The item therefore survives and
        the floating `{#next}` attaches to its heading. The task spelling has
        the same column because the checkbox is content, not marker width; an
        ordered `1.` marker puts the equivalent heading at column 3.

        Move those headings one column left and S4 gives the opposite answer.
        A floating attribute opens no paragraph, so the partial prefix match
        closes the item and re-classifies the heading-shaped residue as literal
        paragraph text in the document context. The pending attribute is scoped
        to the item (§15 A4), becomes dangling when that item closes, and MUST
        NOT escape to the document paragraph. Ordinary marker-line text is the
        control: it opens a paragraph, so a below-column line lazily folds into
        it. A task item keeps its authored checkbox even when no block remains;
        zero width in the column calculation does not erase task state. This is
        the same open-paragraph rule on both sides; attributes and task
        checkboxes do not manufacture wider list markers.

        The question is asked of a QUOTE recursively. `- > q` / `tail` folds
        because the quote ends on an open paragraph; `- > # H` / `tail` does
        not, because it ends on a heading. Non-emptiness is not the test - a
        quote is a container like any other and its own last block answers.

        A QUOTE'S LAST BLOCK MAY ITSELF BE A QUOTE -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#1355). "Recursively" has no depth in it, and neither does "a
        container like any other". An OUTER quote whose last block is an inner
        quote is answered by what the INNER quote ends on:

            > > # H          > > | a |          > > ---
            tail             > > | b |          tail
                             tail

        The rule reaches blocks written at an item's CONTENT COLUMN too. A
        heading there leaves no paragraph open, so a following below-column
        line cannot stay in that item. In a nested list the owner table is then
        applied to the surviving frames: corpus 75-list-nesting-and-looseness-4
        closes the inner item and reclassifies `lazy` in the outer item, whose
        paragraph remains the selected owner. This is ownership-first S4, not a
        depth exception.

        A WRAPPED attribute block (`- {.a` / `  .b}` / `tail`) is one
        interrupting attribute line, not a paragraph split into physical-line
        fragments. The opener and every continuation line leave no paragraph
        open, so `tail` is reclassified outside the item. Whether the closing
        brace shares the opener's line cannot change the interruption rule.

        A DEFINITION AT THE CONTENT COLUMN ENDS THE PARAGRAPH, NOT THE
        CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE (carve#1350). One part of the open half above
        is settled, and by §10 I5 rather than by analogy. A link or footnote
        definition written at a list item's `content_column`, or at a
        definition body's, is an INTERRUPTER: it ends the paragraph it sits
        under. Nothing about it closes the container. The container ends
        because the NEXT line arrives at column 0 with no open paragraph left
        anywhere in the stack, which is S4's own "otherwise" and nothing new:

            - a                :: t
              [r]: /u          : a
            tail                 [r]: /u
                              tail

        both end their container and publish `tail` at document level. A
        COMMENT at a definition body's content column ends the paragraph the
        same way and for the same reason (§10 I5 lists it too), so
        `: a` over `  %% c` over `tail` ends the `dd`.

        AND THE DEFINITION REGISTERS, which is I5's other half: "A link or
        footnote definition belongs to an open list item only at that item's
        `content_column`". Ending the container while DROPPING the definition
        is not conforming.
        I5's other two columns are the controls and are UNCHANGED: at DOCUMENT
        column 0 the definition is a document-level interrupter, and at a
        nonzero column BELOW `content_column` it is lazy paragraph text that
        does not register at all -- `- a` / ` [r]: /u` / `tail` folds, with the
        definition line published as text.

        An ABBREVIATION definition is I5's SECOND exception (§16, and I5's AN
        INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION): recognized at document level
        only, so inside a container the line is not an invisible line at all --
        it is ordinary paragraph text and reopens the paragraph like any other
        prose.

        A QUOTE IS REACHED BY ITS MARKER, AND A COLUMN NEVER REACHES INTO ONE
        (carve#1384). I5's columns are read against the container a line is IN,
        and a line that writes no `>` is in no quote whatever column it lands
        on -- §24 C5 composes the strips and the quote's strip IS the marker
        (carve#1368). Such a line reaches the quote only through S4's lazy fold,
        so it is paragraph text: it renders where it was written and registers
        nothing.

        WITH THE MARKER WRITTEN nothing about I5 changes: `> - x` over
        `>   [r]: /u` registers at the inner item's content column. The marker
        is the parameter, and the quote's body is not.

        A NEW MARKER DOES NOT REACH A DEAD CONTAINER'S COLUMN -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#1892). A blank line ends a
        block quote, and every column opened INSIDE it dies with it, including
        the item columns. A later line that writes the marker again opens a NEW
        quote; it does not re-enter the old one, and it inherits nothing from
        it. So

            > - x

            >   [r]: /u

            > [r][]

        has no item open when `[r]: /u` is read. The definition sits two columns
        ABOVE the new quote's content column with nothing between, and I5's
        first control asks for the content column exactly: `> - x` over
        `>   [r]: /u` registers because an item is open at that column, and the
        same two lines with a blank between them do not, because none is. The
        definition is PARAGRAPH TEXT -- published where it was written, and
        registering nothing, so `[r][]` stays literal.

        ABOVE A CONTENT COLUMN IS NOT AT IT, which is the general rule that
        control is one case of. I5's three columns are exhaustive as written:
        AT `content_column` the definition registers, at DOCUMENT column 0 it
        registers, and at a nonzero column BELOW `content_column` it is lazy
        paragraph text. A column ABOVE `content_column` with no item open is
        none of the three, and it resolves the way the document level resolves
        it: a container is a document with a margin, and indentation above the
        margin is no more a definition site than column 2 is at the top level.
        The single exception stays the one §24 C3 already carves out -- WITH AN
        ITEM OPEN the test is "at or past" rather than "exactly at", because the
        item erases an authored base before it parses the line (carve#1705).
        With no item open there is no base to erase, and with more than one
        open the clause below says which of them the test is measured from.

        AT OR PAST MEANS THE DEEPEST COLUMN THE LINE REACHES -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#1896). Containers nest, so their content columns do too, and a
        definition can land strictly BETWEEN two open ones. The test is read
        against the deepest column the LINE REACHES, never against the deepest
        container left OPEN: the definition registers in the innermost open
        container whose content column it is at or past, and a container it
        does not reach neither owns the line nor withholds it. So

            - - x
               [r]: /url

            See [r][].

        opens items at content columns 2 and 4, and the definition at column 3
        registers against the outer item -- as it does at 2, at 4 and at 5.
        Moving that one line between the columns changes nothing a reader can
        see. No quote is needed to state the rule and none is a parameter of
        it: `- > - - x` over `  >    [r]: /url` is the same shape wearing a
        marker, and answers the same way. All four columns are pinned by corpus
        441-a-definition-between-two-open-content-columns-reaches-the-outer-one,
        the neighbours with the divergent one, because the neighbours are what
        make the rule legible.

        THE OTHER READING LEAVES A HOLE, which is what settles it. Read as the
        deepest container still OPEN, column 3 is not at or past column 4, so
        the line folds and defines nothing while columns 2 and 4 both register:
        one added space removes a definition and a second restores it. Past a
        container's content column more indentation may change WHICH container
        owns a line; it never changes whether the line is a definition at all.
        Nor can the deeper item be the owner. A line at column 3 does not REACH
        the item at column 4 -- that is what reaching means -- so that item
        could only take the line by lazy folding, and §10 I5 spends it first:
        at a container's content column a definition INTERRUPTS the open
        paragraph and registers, rather than folding into it. The container the
        line does reach is the item at column 2.

        BELOW EVERY OPEN COLUMN IS UNCHANGED. This clause governs a line that
        reaches one open content column and not the next. A definition at a
        nonzero column below the OUTERMOST open content column reaches no
        opener column at all; it stays lazy paragraph text of the container
        whose column it fell below (§24 C3, AN INVISIBLE LINE FOLDS LIKE ANY
        OTHER), and this clause does not admit it.

        THIS IS THE MARKED SPELLING OF A RULE ALREADY SETTLED for the unmarked
        one. `> - a` over a blank over four spaces reaches column 4 and is
        inside NOTHING, so it registers nothing while the page prints it as
        text (carve-php#1431). Re-marking the quote changes which container the
        line is in; it does not revive the item that ended at the blank.

        PROSE REOPENS THE PARAGRAPH WHEREVER IT SITS AMONG THE ITEM'S BLOCKS,
        which is the general shape of that last sentence rather than a property
        of abbreviations. S4 asks whether any container in the open stack holds
        an OPEN paragraph. It does not ask whether that paragraph is the
        container's first block, and an item whose first block is a table, a
        fence or a quote and whose next line is prose holds one exactly as an
        item that began with prose does. So

            - | a |          - ```            - > | a |
              b                c                + b |
            tail               ```            tail
                               b
                               tail

        all fold `tail` into the paragraph the prose line opened, as `- a` /
        `tail` does. The blocks before that paragraph are spent: they
        answered S4 while they were the item's last block and stopped answering
        it when prose reopened one.

        AN UNTERMINATED CONTAINER DOES NOT EXTEND THE ITEM PAST THAT BLANK.
        The control above says the blank closes the paragraph so the item ends,
        and that holds whatever container stands above the blank. S4 asks
        whether an OPEN PARAGRAPH exists, not whether a container is still
        waiting for its closer, so a line BELOW the item's content column after
        a blank has nothing to continue and leaves the item -- under a
        terminated `:::` div, an opaque body, a quote, or no container at all,
        and under an unterminated `:::` div identically. So

            - ::: d          - ::: d          - ::: d
              b                b                b
                               :::
            tail                              (no blank)
                             tail             tail

        end the item in the first two spellings and fold in the third, and the
        difference between them is the blank, never the missing closer. A
        following line at the item's content column is the div's second block
        rather than the end of the item. (carve#1379)

        THE COLUMN IS REACHED BY COMPOSING THE STRIPS, NOT BY WALKING THE
        PREFIX (carve#1368). Which container's `content_column` a line reaches
        is decided by §24 C5 and by nothing else: every container strips its
        own prefix and hands the residue down, so the question is asked of the
        INNERMOST container in the coordinates IT was handed. The shape of the
        prefix above it -- how many quotes and list items it alternates, in
        what order, and how deep -- is not a parameter.

        The DROP is the disappearance §24 C3 already forbids under AN INVISIBLE
        LINE FOLDS LIKE ANY OTHER: a definition may register, or it may stay
        text, and half-folding it -- gone from the page, absent from the
        tables -- is not an option the rule offers.

        AT A CONTAINER'S CONTENT COLUMN, A BLOCK ENDS THE PARAGRAPH IT SITS
        UNDER -- NORMATIVE (carve#1350, carve#1357). The definition and the
        comment above are instances; the rule is stated as a property because
        the instances are not enumerable.

        §24 C3 says the content column IS the container body's column 0: a
        block opener is recognized there exactly as one is recognized only at
        column 0 at the top level. So a line there is read
        as a BLOCK, and a block ends the paragraph above it. WHAT THE BLOCK
        RENDERS IS NOT A PARAMETER. A comment, a link or footnote definition
        and an attribute block all render nothing and all three end the
        paragraph; none of them closes the container. The container ends
        because the FOLLOWING line is at DOCUMENT column 0 with no open
        paragraph anywhere in the stack to continue -- S4's own "otherwise".

        THE RULE IS OVER THE BLOCK, NOT OVER ITS FIRST LINE. A footnote
        definition's indented BODY continuation is part of that block: the
        footnote parser consumes it and permits no lazy continuation into it,
        so nothing of the container's is open across any of it. `- a` /
        `  [^f]: t` / `    more` / `tail` therefore ends the item exactly as
        `- a` / `  [^f]: t` / `tail` does, and the two spellings of one
        definition stop answering differently (carve#1357). A LINK reference
        definition has no such body - it is one line in Carve, and an indented
        line under it is ordinary container text in every reader, which is the
        control that says this is about the block's extent and not about
        indentation.

        A HEADING IS THE VISIBLE CONTROL -- NORMATIVE (carve#1377). At the
        content column it is the item's own heading block, and it leaves no
        paragraph open. A following line at document column 0 therefore ends
        the item under S4; it cannot lazily continue the heading or a paragraph
        that does not exist. This is the same answer as a heading on the item
        marker line and a heading inside a quote. The line's position changes
        which container owns the heading, not whether the heading is a block.

        BELOW the content column at a NONZERO column, the following line still
        reaches the container only through S4's lazy fold; §24 C3's comment
        exception keeps that path open. DOCUMENT column 0 is instead the
        enclosing context's block position, and a line AT the content column
        after the block remains the container's second paragraph.

        THE BLOCK'S EXTENT IS THE DEFINITION'S, BLANK LINES AND ALL
        (carve#1363). A footnote definition may carry more than one block, and
        the container's reach over it is the DEFINITION'S extent rather than a
        CONTIGUOUS indented run. The blank between the note's blocks is
        INTERIOR to the block the container is looking at, so it hands nothing
        back:

            - a
              [^f]: t

                more
            tail

        ends the item exactly as the contiguous and the one-line spellings do.
        `tail` is a document-level paragraph and the note holds two blocks.

        THE LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION IS THE CONTROL, and it does not move. It
        has no body, so its block IS the one line: the blank after it falls
        OUTSIDE the block, the indented line below is the container's own
        second paragraph, and the flush-left line folds into that. So this
        turns on the block's extent, not on indentation and not on blank
        lines. The NESTED spelling is not decided here.

        A FENCED BODY IS NOT A PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE (carve#646). The same
        rule, with the unmatched container's last block a CODE FENCE opened on
        the marker line itself:

            - ```
            x
            ```

        `x` supplies none of the body's indentation, so S1 MATCH PREFIXES stops
        at the ITEM and S2 FENCED BODY never fires -- S2 applies only when the
        innermost MATCHED container is a fenced body. S4 governs, its lazy
        branch asks for an open PARAGRAPH, and a verbatim body is not one: there
        is no meaning to "fold in as lazy paragraph text" inside content that is
        not markup. So the containers close, the item holds an EMPTY code block,
        and `x` re-parses at document level -- where the trailing delimiter run
        is ordinary inline text.

        FENCE KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE.
        VERBATIMNESS is not the property doing the work. The reach of a
        container is not extended by what its innermost block happens to be,
        and the parameter S4 consults is whether ANY container in the open
        stack holds an OPEN PARAGRAPH. The fence kind never enters, so the two
        fence kinds are ONE rule and the asymmetry between them falls out of
        the paragraph: a CODE fence body cannot hold an open paragraph at all,
        so the column-0 line always takes S4's otherwise and the container
        ends, while a COLON fence body CAN hold one and takes the lazy branch
        when it does.

        AND A FENCE THAT OPENED NO BODY IS NOT ONE EITHER (carve#1387). The
        first bullet presumes a code fence body EXISTS. §10 I4 decides that, and
        it is a question about the CLOSER: a fence interrupts an open paragraph
        only when one follows it. Without a closer the line is ordinary
        paragraph text, which is the answer every reader already gives at
        document level for

            q
            ```
            b

        -- one paragraph holding an unclosed inline verbatim run, not a code
        block. So at a container's content column

            - q                 > q                 : a
              ```               > ```                 ```
            tail                tail                tail

        THE CLOSER LOOKAHEAD DOES NOT STOP AT A BELOW-COLUMN LINE. §24 C3 folds
        such a line as lazy text while a paragraph is open, so a closer written
        below it is still written inside the container. Stopping there makes the
        question circular -- the fence opens only if the line folds, and the
        line folds only if the fence did not open -- and it answers

            - a
              ```
              b
             y
              ```

        wrongly: the item ends at ` y`, because this fence does have its closer
        and a real body is open.

        A BLANK LINE and a TERMINATED fence are the controls, and both are
        unchanged: a blank closes the paragraph so S4's otherwise governs
        (carve#1379), and a fence with a closer is a BLOCK whose body leaves no
        paragraph open, which is the first bullet with its premise intact.

        A LAZY FOLD KEEPS THE CONTAINER OPEN FOR FOLLOWING LINES -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#980). S4's lazy branch says NOTHING closes, and that binds the
        lines AFTER the folded one as much as the folded one itself. A line
        that returns to the container's content column is still that
        container's content:

            - x                 - x
              :::                 :::
              a                   a
            d                   d
              b
              :::

        On the left `a`, `d` and `b` are one paragraph in one div; `b` MUST NOT
        land beside the div. The right-hand document is the same rule with the
        fold as the last line. The CONTROL: with nothing in the stack open, the
        flush-left line still ENDS the container and the line after it is
        outside too.

        A REAL DIV IS A CONTAINER LIKE ANY OTHER -- NORMATIVE (carve#909). An
        unterminated `::: ` div in a container decides the same way, on the same
        question, and the two answers differ by one line of body:

            - item              - item
              ::: note            ::: note
              body              tail
            tail

        On the left the div holds an OPEN PARAGRAPH, so `tail` folds into it.
        On the right the div is EMPTY -- and the item's own paragraph was closed
        by the div that followed it -- so nothing in the stack is open, the
        containers close and `tail` is a top-level paragraph. Closing the div
        with a `:::` line closes the paragraph inside it and inverts the left
        answer, which is what the third document pins.

        CONTAINER KIND DOES NOT DETERMINE CONTAINER REACH -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#920). The same three documents written with a BLOCK QUOTE decide
        the same way. `> quote` / `> ::: note` / `tail` closes the quote and
        leaves `tail` at top level, and so does the CLOSED form
        `> quote` / `> ::: note` / `> body` / `> :::` / `tail`, because a closed
        container holds no open paragraph any more than an empty one does.

        DEFINITION BODIES FOLLOW THE SAME CONTAINER REACH RULE -- NORMATIVE
        (carve#956). The third indented-block collector answers the same way. A
        definition
        body's canonical `dd` demands indentation to column 2
        (`definition_indent`), so a fence opened on the `: ` marker line whose
        body sits BELOW that column
        is the list spelling above with a different prefix:

            :: t
            : ```
            body
            ```

        `body` supplies none of the body's indentation, so S1 MATCH PREFIXES
        stops at the DEFINITION ENTRY and S2 FENCED BODY never fires. S4
        governs, its lazy branch asks for an open PARAGRAPH, and a verbatim
        body is not one. So the containers close, the `dd` holds an EMPTY code
        block, and `body` re-parses at document level -- where the trailing
        delimiter run is ordinary inline text, byte for byte the answer the
        `- ``` ` spelling gets.

     S5 OPENERS. When S3/S4 classify the residue as a container opener (a
        quote marker; a list marker at an admissible column; a `+`
        continuation marker at the marker column, PART 9 §17 L3/L4; a fence
        opener whose closer-lookahead guard holds), push the container and
        recurse on the remaining residue.
   OUTPUT. The layout-resolved line stream (equivalently: INDENT / DEDENT /
           CONT events plus line residues). PARTS 1-2 parse blocks over it;
           inline parsing (PART 3) runs per finished block -- the two-phase
           model of PART 8.
   ============================================================================ *)
